A||||4||Garet Jax||Hello, great forum for clutch info :)||||1||
Z||000000||Garet Jax||09-11-2000||03:13 AM||||Hi!<BR>I have been reading all the clutch related info as I have recently ran into some clutch issues with my 84 4-speed.<BR>I thought I would try to add a little to this great repository of info.<BR>Drove through a large puddle (small lake?) last week during some serious storms in the area trying to make it home. After the puddle, from a stop the car would seem to jump as the clutch engaged. I thought I had overheated it or burnt it a little slipping it through the water. Got home parked it in the garage, this was Friday evening. Monday went to go to work and depressed the pedal, put it in reverse, started the car and it launched out of the gargage. So I called work and took the day off to tinker.<BR>Clutch pedal was first on the list, since mine was below the level of the brake pedal.<BR>Didn't fix it.<BR>Checked hydraulics, every thing ok, an inch of travel on the slave cylinder rod.<BR>Decided to pull the clutch thinking bad pressure plate and here the cool part.<BR>You can remove/replace the cluth without dropping the cradle!<P>Here's what I did<BR>Unhook shift cables, slave cylinder, etc...<BR>Support engine<BR>Unbolt tranny mounts from tranny and cradle<BR>Remove tranny mounts<BR>Remove drivers rear wheel and disconnect lower A-arm<BR>Pull out on strut brake assy till halfshaft comes out and can be moved out of the way<BR>Unbolt tranny and slide it back till it hits fenderwell<BR>This gives about 4-6 inches of room to remove and replace clutch.<BR>Whole removal process took about 1 hour 15 mins.<P>Anywho, I got it apart and stuck my hand in there and with some effort could depress diaphragm fingers with my fingers, not a good sign.<BR>So old clutch is out new clutch goes in tomorrow.<P>Thanks for the great reading and great forum!<BR>Any of you fabricators out there stuck an old BOP 235 aluminium V-8 in a Fiero yet? Should weigh about as much as an Iron Duke and they used to make some power, some were even turboed. Any info on this would be appreciated as I have one laying around  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||yobtah||09-11-2000||09:51 AM||||I may do something like this soon, so I'm curious... how did you support the engine?  Did you use one of the actual engine support braces from GM (or a custom one)? Or did you support it from the bottom?||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||09-11-2000||12:59 PM||||Well, you got my attention with this one.  I have two Fieros with clutches that need attention, so if it's possible to work on them without dropping the cradle, I'm all ears  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  It sounds as if you were able to leave the strut attached, so no realignment needed.<P>I take it you were able to remove the pressure plate & disc already.  Hope the installation works out well, also.  Any trouble changing the throwout bearing, too?<P>When you said you slid the transaxle outboard, I kind of pictured that you used guidepins.  I assume that it might even be possible to get the flywheel out, too.<P>I'll be interested in reading about how things work out.  Photos would also be appreciated, as always!  Good luck!         ||||reg||10
Z||000003||Philphine||09-12-2000||01:50 AM||||back around the beginning of this year (or the end of last) hot rod mag had a vega with this engine. the builder had used some old turbo pieces <BR>(i think the olds engine had the turbo) with a t-bird turbo. done by a company called rance engineering. also in hot rod mar. '85, there is an article on this engine. included were a couple of parts sources (don't know if they'd still be good) and info on building it up to 305 ci. i know neither of these mention fieros but ever since i read about it i have been wondering too. it looks like the cheapest (if you can find one) aluminum v8 route. ||||reg||1
